nd tests as required, even if it means covering fewer requirements upfront. I need to respect the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, even though the project's differential testing approach makes them feel intertwined. So I'm structuring this with alternating implementation and test tasks, where each test task depends on its corresponding implementation task, and the next implementation task depends on completing the tests first.\n\nSince both Task 0 and Task 2 modify main.go, they can't run in parallel without merge conflicts—Task 2 needs to wait for Task 0 to complete. This creates a linear dependency chain: Task 0 → Task 1 → Task 2 → Task 3.\n\nNow I'm laying out the actual task descriptions, making sure each one is self-contained and references the SRD file, existing code files like main.go and ls_test.go, and follows the project's patterns.\n\nThe ls command depends on pkg/sys and pkg/format according to the architecture, so the stitch agent will need to account for those dependencies when it processes the existing code—though pkg/format might not be started yet, the earlier tasks probably handled this already. Looking at the requirements breakdown, R1.9-R1.12 likely covers long format and display options, while R1.13-R1.14 are remaining core features, and the R2 group ties into the extended and sort features from the roadmap.\n\nI'm drafting task titles that map to the SRD requirements more precisely: core display features for R1.9-R1.12, then extended display and sort flags for R1.13-R1.14 and R2.1-R2.2, with corresponding test tasks for each. But I'm working from assumptions about what those requirement ranges actually contain, so I should check the descriptive names from the earlier tasks to make sure I'm matching the SRD content accurately.\n\nLooking at the rel04.0 roadmap, the full ls implementation breaks down into core flags (default, -1, -l, -a, -A, -d, --color, -h) covered in the R1 group, which has 14 total items